uperior pulmonary sulcus tumors and Pancoast's syndrome. > Etiology\n  Pancoast syndrome, the characteristic constellation of shoulder and arm pain, Horner syndrome, and hand muscle weakness, is most commonly caused by apical lung cancer but is not exclusive to this etio\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → Steroid-induced hypercoagulable state → Vascular/Ischemic Upper-Limb Deficit\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Assess for hypercoagulability (e.g., D-dimer, coagulation profile) given anabolic steroid use and consider vascular imaging (e.g., MRI/MRA) for brachial artery thrombosis\n  The patient has a medical history of diabetes. He uses anabolic steroids. He has lost 17 pounds since he last came to the emergency department 1 month ago → Paraneoplastic syndrome → Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy (e.g., Lymphoma-Related)\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for occult malignancy (e.g., lymphoma workup with imaging and labs) given unexplained weight loss and asymmetric brachial plexus involvement\n  The patient has a medical history of diabetes. He drinks 2-7 alcoholic drinks per day and has smoked 2 packs of cigarettes per day since he was 25. He has lost 17 pounds since he last came to the emergency department 1 month ago → Apical lung tumor → Pancoast Tumor (Apical Lung Carcinoma with Brachial Plexus Involvement)\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Order chest CT to exclude apical lung mass given heavy smoking, weight loss, and progressive right arm weakness (Pancoast tumor classic presentation)\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
